<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trentino</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Province of Trento</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trentino is a mountainous region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a> as its capital, today an autonomous province. For most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period much of it was ruled by the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Trent. Secularised in 1803, it then belonged to the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> until 1918, when Italy occupied it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; the annexation was confirmed in 1919.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trentino.jpg" alt="Trentino" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trentino_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Trentino, officially the Autonomous Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a>, lies in the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and covers 6,207 square kilometres. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> it forms the autonomous region of Trentino-Alto Adige/Südtirol. The province is mountainous and contains much of the Dolomites, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, whose highest peak, the Marmolada, rises to 3,343 metres. The Adige River flows through its centre, and its valley, where the principal towns lie, has long connected Italy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. The name derives from the capital, Trento, and originally referred only to the city and its surroundings. Under Austrian rule the common German name was Welschtirol, or Foreign Tyrol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The valleys were inhabited from the mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the area belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-verona">March of Verona</a>. In 1027 Emperor Conrad II established the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> as a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, governed by prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>, held in three sessions between 1545 and 1563, set out Roman Catholic doctrine in response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the bishopric was secularised and absorbed into the Austrian County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>. Trentino lay on the front line between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and saw heavy fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Italy occupied it in November 1918 and it was formally ceded under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye</a> in 1919. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Gruber-De Gasperi Agreement created the autonomous region, and a second Statute of Autonomy in 1972 devolved most powers to the two provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Trentino has more than half a million people and 166 municipalities. Italian predominates, but three indigenous minorities, speakers of Ladin, Mòcheno and Cimbrian, are protected by law. The province has its own elected government and a council of 35 members, one of whom must by law come from the Ladin minority. Since 1996 it has belonged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> and the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> to a Euroregion whose boundaries match the old County of Tyrol. Its castles include Castel del Buonconsiglio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a> and Castel Thun in the Non Valley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trentino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many Italian speakers in <b>Trentino</b> and Venezia Giulia remained outside the kingdom, which gave rise to Italian irredentism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Italy concluded a secret agreement with the United Kingdom, France and Russia, promising to enter the First World War on the Entente side within a month in return for <b>Trentino</b>, South Tyrol, Trieste, Istria, part of Dalmatia and other gains.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-26">26 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory covered much of modern <b>Trentino</b> and the upper Adige.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-trent">Duchy of Trent</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1918 <b>Trentino</b> became part of Italy, and the see remains as the Archdiocese of Trento.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-trent">Prince-Bishopric of Trent</a></li></ul></section>
